WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.521
External data often controls sizes, indexes, offsets, element counts, or serialized lengths.

2
00:00:04.521 --> 00:00:10.926
Values can come from files, protocols, APIs, command-line arguments, environment variables, configuration, databases, or user interfaces.

3
00:00:10.926 --> 00:00:15.824
They should be considered untrusted until the program has parsed and validated them.

4
00:00:15.824 --> 00:00:21.852
Validation should happen before the value influences allocation, indexing, copying, transformation, or parsing of later fields.

5
00:00:21.852 --> 00:00:27.880
A length in a file header does not prove that the file contains that many bytes.

6
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.778
An index in a request does not prove that the referenced element exists.

7
00:00:32.778 --> 00:00:38.430
A count in configuration does not prove that the service should allocate that much memory.

8
00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:41.067
Defensive maximums are part of bounds safety.

9
00:00:41.067 --> 00:00:49.733
Even when a number fits in the machine type, it may be too large for the application, tenant, device, request, or resource budget.

10
00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:55.761
Clear caps prevent unreasonable work and give operators predictable failure behavior when input is outside policy.

11
00:00:55.761 --> 00:00:57.268
Cross-field consistency matters.

12
00:00:57.268 --> 00:01:00.282
A record count should match the available records.

13
00:01:00.282 --> 00:01:03.673
A declared length should fit within the remaining data.

14
00:01:03.673 --> 00:01:06.687
An offset should point into the expected region.

15
00:01:06.687 --> 00:01:09.324
A unit should be recognized before conversion.

16
00:01:09.324 --> 00:01:13.092
Values that are individually plausible can still be inconsistent together.

17
00:01:13.092 --> 00:01:15.729
Keep parsing, validation, and memory access separate.

18
00:01:15.729 --> 00:01:25.525
The code should first turn bytes or text into a value, then check syntax and domain rules, then convert into the application type, then access memory

19
00:01:25.525 --> 00:01:26.656
or perform work.

20
00:01:26.656 --> 00:01:30.800
Separating those steps makes safe failure easier to test and review.
